{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And these are the garments that they shall make: the breastpiece and the ephod.</b> Here he mentioned the חֹשֶׁן (breastpiece) before the אֵפוֹד (ephod), and afterward, when detailing them, he put the אֵפוֹד first and then 'וְעָשִׂיתָ חֹשֶׁן' (Exodus 28:15). And likewise at the time of their actual making, in parashat Pekudei, he also put the אֵפוֹד before the חֹשֶׁן (Exodus 39:2). And there is a further difficulty: in the case of all the others it says 'וְעָשִׂיתָ' (you shall make), whereas here it says 'וְעָשׂוּ אֶת הָאֵפוֹד' (Exodus 28:6). And I marvel at all the commentators who raised the question about what is said regarding the ark — 'וְעָשׂוּ אֲרוֹן' (Exodus 25:10) — yet here, not a mouth opens. According to what will be explained shortly regarding the making of the אֵפוֹד, that it comes for atonement for idolatrous worship — namely, for the calf that they made, which caused the breaking of the tablets that Moses carried on his shoulders — therefore the אֵפוֹד rests upon his two shoulders, as it is written: 'שְׁתֵּי כְתֵפוֹת חֹבְרוֹת יִהְיֶה לּוֹ' (Exodus 28:7), corresponding to the two tablets upon which there were five commandments opposite five. If so, it is fitting that it says 'וְעָשׂוּ' in the plural, for the same reason that 'וְעָשׂוּ' is said regarding the ark." "It does indeed seem plausible to hear, for the Sages said in tractate Arakhin (16a): why were sacrifices juxtaposed to the priestly garments? To tell you: just as sacrifices atone, so too priestly garments atone. The חֹשֶׁן atones for corruption of justice; the אֵפוֹד atones for idolatrous worship; the מְעִיל atones for slander; the כְּתֹנֶת atones for bloodshed; the מִצְנֶפֶת atones for arrogance; the אַבְנֵט atones for improper thoughts of the heart; the צִיץ atones for brazenness; the מִכְנָסַיִם atones for uncovering of nakedness — and he derives all of them there from Scripture. According to this, we can explain why the Holy One, blessed be He, placed the חֹשֶׁן before the אֵפוֹד: for the Holy One, blessed be He, is more insistent about the corruption of justice than about idolatrous worship. And a proof: from the generation of the Dispersion, who stretched out their hands against the Essence Himself yet were not destroyed; and the generation of the Flood — because there was violence and robbery among them — they were destroyed. For if a man sins against HaShem, blessed be He, what can He do to him? But Israel, for the honor of the Place, blessed be He, preceded the making of the אֵפוֹד, for the sin of idolatrous worship is graver to them than the corruption of justice. Therefore it says 'וְעָשׂוּ אֶת הָאֵפוֹד,' for the language of 'וְעָשׂוּ' also conveys a future declaration, to say that thus it would be — that they would make the אֵפוֹד first. And furthermore: the Holy One, blessed be He, has it in His hand to forgive and to remit idolatrous worship, but not the corruption of justice in matters between a man and his fellow, for the Holy One, blessed be He, forgives only in matters that are between Him and His creatures. But Israel said: it is in our hands to forgive and to remit what happened to us through the corruption of justice; but the share of the Most High — who can remit? Therefore they preceded the making of the אֵפוֹד." "And this resembles what the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: 'Avenge the vengeance of the children of Israel from the Midianites' (Numbers 31:2), while Moses said: 'To execute the vengeance of HaShem upon Midian.' From here Rashi learns to say: even though Moses heard that his death was dependent on this matter, nevertheless he acted with joy and did not delay. For had he not acted with joy, he should have said to Israel the matter as it was — that they should avenge themselves against them, the vengeance of the children of Israel for what they did to them, since they brought down twenty-four thousand of Israel. Rather, surely Moses reasoned: since Israel loves its leaders, and they will hear that the death of Moses depends on this vengeance, if so they will say: 'Does the Holy One, blessed be He, really seek to take vengeance — is it not on our account?' If so, Israel will say: 'We forgive what they did to us, and we will not seek vengeance so hastily.' Therefore Moses changed the wording and said: 'To execute the vengeance of HaShem upon Midian' — for what they did against HaShem in the matter of Peor; and if so, the share of the Most High — who can remit after Him? And if so, Moses surely acted with joy and did not delay. And this matter will be explained further in parashat Mattot with the help of HaShem." "<b>The breastpiece and the ephod and the robe, etc.</b> He did not mention the צִיץ and the מִכְנָסַיִם, because they are not included in the category of vestment, for they are not garments. But since we find here three instances of the language of making: 'וְעָשִׂיתָ בִגְדֵי קֹדֶשׁ לְאַהֲרֹן אָחִיךָ' (Exodus 28:2), and afterward it says 'וְעָשׂוּ אֶת בִגְדֵי אַהֲרֹן לְקַדְּשׁוֹ' (Exodus 28:3), and afterward it says 'וְאֵלֶּה הַבְּגָדִים אֲשֶׁר יַעֲשׂוּ' (Exodus 28:4) — we can say that the first and the second, since holiness is mentioned in them, surely contain an allusion to the צִיץ and the מִכְנָסַיִם, for wherever you find a safeguarding of nakedness, there you find holiness. And according to the view of our Sages, of blessed memory (Arakhin 16a), the מִכְנָסַיִם atones for sexual immorality, and I add and say that the צִיץ too atones for sexual immorality — one in hiddenness and one in the open. For the מִכְנָסַיִם, which is in a place of concealment, atones for matters of concealment — that is, one who goes after sexual immorality in stealth and covering, as it is written: 'לְכַסּוֹת בְּשַׂר עֶרְוָה' (Exodus 28:42). And the צִיץ atones for brazenness of forehead — that is, one who transgresses openly without shame — and this is what the Gemara brings there (ibid. 16a) with the verse: 'וּמֵצַח אִשָּׁה זוֹנָה הָיָה לָךְ' (Jeremiah 3:3). Therefore it is written on the צִיץ: 'קֹדֶשׁ לַה׳.' And even if we say that the צִיץ atones for brazenness of forehead in general, nevertheless the first act of making alludes to the צִיץ, upon which is written 'קֹדֶשׁ לַה׳.'" "And with this, what was said earlier is resolved: 'וְעָשִׂיתָ בִגְדֵי קֹדֶשׁ לְאַהֲרֹן' (Exodus 28:2) — he placed 'holy garments' before 'Aharon'; and afterward he said: 'וְעָשׂוּ אֶת בִגְדֵי אַהֲרֹן לְקַדְּשׁוֹ' (Exodus 28:3) — he placed 'the garments of Aharon' before 'the holiness.' For the first verse speaks of the צִיץ, for previously it had not been his practice to wear any צִיץ; therefore it says 'וְעָשִׂיתָ בִגְדֵי קֹדֶשׁ לְאַהֲרֹן' — that he should make for Aharon a garment inscribed with the engraving of a seal, 'קֹדֶשׁ לַה׳,' and it shall be to him 'כְּבֶגֶד יַעֲטֶה' (Psalms 109:19). But below, it speaks of the מִכְנָסַיִם, for in any case it is the way of human beings to go clothed in מִכְנָסַיִם; therefore it says 'וְעָשׂוּ אֶת בִגְדֵי אַהֲרֹן לְקַדְּשׁוֹ' — that is to say: those garments of Aharon which he was already accustomed to wear, now they shall make them to sanctify him from sexual immorality."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:24:12.640626265Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}